Security audit follow-up requiring operator/policy review.

The `skills/re-frame2-implementor` audit found two workflow-level trust concerns:
- the skill can perform cross-repo `bd create` side effects without a distinct explicit approval gate;
- it assumes a mutable, unpinned upstream spec checkout as canonical input.

Action needed:
- decide the approval model for side effects outside the current repo/workspace;
- decide the provenance/pinning requirements for upstream spec inputs used by implementation skills;
- align the skill docs and workflow to that decision.

Operator review is requested because this is a cross-repo workflow trust decision.

Security audit follow-up requiring operator/policy review.

The `skills/re-frame2-setup` audit found three policy-level concerns:
- published skills currently permit overly broad shell wildcards (`npm *`, `npx *`, etc.);
- installation guidance promotes mutable/unpinned repo content into executable skill code;
- nREPL attachment guidance lacks a standard localhost/dev-only warning.

Action needed:
- define least-privilege standards for `allowed-tools` in published skills;
- define provenance/pinning expectations for skill installation;
- define standard warning language for nREPL/live-code-attachment guidance.

Operator review is requested because this is a repo-wide skill publication/install policy question.

Security audit follow-up requiring spec/operator review.

The `tools/story-mcp` audit found two contract-level questions:
- whether `:include-sensitive?` is an unrestricted per-call bypass or an operator-controlled capability;
- whether `register-variant` is intended to accept raw EDN string bodies or only structured/validated inputs.

Action needed:
- define the authority model for sensitive reads in Story MCP;
- decide the intended write-input contract for `register-variant`;
- align docs/tests/implementation to that decision.

Operator review is requested because this affects privacy guarantees and the public API surface.

Security audit follow-up requiring spec/operator review.

The `tools/story` audit found two default third-party egress paths:
- QR sharing sends full share URLs, including variant override data, to `api.qrserver.com`;
- the a11y panel loads `axe-core` from a public CDN at runtime.

Action needed:
- decide whether Story is allowed to contact third-party services by default in developer sessions;
- if yes, define the privacy/CSP/allowlist expectations;
- if not, move these features to local/self-hosted implementations and align the spec.

Operator review is requested because this is a tooling-networking policy decision.

Security audit follow-up requiring spec/operator review.

The `tools/pair2-mcp` audit found two contract issues:
- whether `dispatch` is intentionally allowed to be equivalent to arbitrary code execution, or must stay constrained to event-vector dispatch;
- whether direct-read privacy for `snapshot` / `get-path` / `sub-cache` must match the canonical Tool-Pair elision contract.

Action needed:
- decide the intended authority boundary for `dispatch` versus `eval-cljs`;
- reconcile direct-read privacy semantics with `spec/Tool-Pair.md`;
- align docs/tests/implementation to that decision.

Operator review is requested because this is a security-boundary decision for a high-authority tool.

Security audit follow-up requiring spec/operator review.

The `tools/mcp-base` audit found two shared-contract questions:
- whether the response cap is meant to bound only `:content` text or the full serialized MCP result;
- whether malformed non-boolean `:sensitive?` stamps should pass through or be suppressed at the egress boundary.

Action needed:
- decide the canonical cap/budgeting semantics for shared MCP responses;
- decide the fail-open vs fail-closed posture for malformed sensitivity stamps;
- align shared docs/tests and downstream consumers to that decision.

Operator review is requested because this affects multiple MCP surfaces and their security/privacy contracts.

Security audit follow-up requiring spec/operator review.

The `tools/causa-mcp` audit found two unresolved security-contract questions:
- what authority model applies to mutation tools versus arbitrary `eval-cljs` once an MCP server is enabled;
- what privacy/elision guarantees direct-read tree tools must honor by default.

Action needed:
- define the canonical capability/approval model for `dispatch`, `restore-epoch`, `reset-frame-db`, and `eval-cljs`;
- reconcile the direct-read privacy wording across `004-Wire-Pipeline.md`, `spec/Tool-Pair.md`, and related docs;
- align future implementation and MUST coverage to that decision.

Operator review is requested because this is a tooling/security model decision, not just an implementation detail.

Security audit follow-up requiring spec/operator review.

The `implementation/ssr` audit found that current SSR semantics are underspecified at two trust boundaries:
- whether hostile/invalid DOM tag keywords must be rejected before HTML emission;
- whether SSR itself must validate header names and cookie fields, or whether that responsibility is delegated entirely to host adapters.

Action needed:
- decide the canonical SSR validation boundary for tag names, response headers, and cookies;
- define the required failure mode / error keywords;
- align spec/docs and implementation to that decision.

Operator review is requested because this is an SSR contract/security-boundary decision.

Security audit follow-up requiring spec/operator review.

The `implementation/schemas` audit found three contract-level questions:
- whether boundary validation is allowed to fail open when no validator hook is loaded;
- whether sensitive subscription validation failures must redact `:query-v`;
- whether opaque/non-EDN schema forms are supported for built-in `:sensitive?` / `:large?` extraction.

Action needed:
- decide the canonical behavior for missing validator hookups at security boundaries;
- decide the required redaction semantics for sensitive sub-return failures;
- decide whether opaque schema forms are supported for built-in sensitivity/elision features.

Operator review is requested because these are `/spec` semantics, not only local implementation details.

Security audit follow-up requiring spec/operator review.

The `implementation/ssr-ring` audit found two deployment-contract questions:
- whether whole-`app-db` hydration is allowed as the default payload policy;
- what trust level the default shell options (`:app-element-id`, `:script-src`, `:head`, `:body-end`) are allowed to assume.

Action needed:
- decide the default hydration exposure policy for SSR responses;
- decide whether shell options are trusted-code-only or may come from mutable admin/tenant config;
- align spec/docs and implementation to that decision.

Operator review is requested because this affects the SSR security model and public/default behavior.

Security audit follow-up requiring spec/operator review.

The `implementation/flows` audit found two contract-level questions:
- whether flow runtime state (`flows`, `last-inputs`) must be cleaned up on frame destroy as part of the frame-isolation contract;
- whether flow evaluation failures must hit the always-on error channel in production, not only the dev trace channel.

Action needed:
- decide the required teardown semantics for flow registries and dirty-check caches;
- decide the required production visibility/error-policy behavior for flow evaluation failures;
- align spec/docs and implementation to that decision.

Operator review is requested because this affects `/spec` behavior, not just local cleanup code.

Security audit follow-up requiring spec/operator review.

The `implementation/routing` audit found two security-sensitive contract questions in URL parsing:
- whether query keys/keyword-typed values are meant to be interned from user-controlled URLs;
- what the required behavior is for malformed percent-encoding.

Those behaviors affect both compatibility and the security posture (memory-growth DoS and fail-open vs fail-closed routing behavior).

Action needed:
- decide the canonical treatment of unknown query keys and keyword-typed query values;
- decide the canonical behavior for malformed percent-encoding;
- align spec/docs and implementation to that decision.

Operator review is requested because this is a `/spec` contract decision.

